Read a serialized module's dependencies afresh in Linkage::isBinaryModuleUpToDate.

Linkage::isBinaryModuleUpToDate (source/slang/slang-session.cpp) hashes each file dependency the
module records and compares the result with the module's digest. It reads the files through
Linkage::loadSourceFile, that is, through the session's file system, a CacheFileSystem that keeps
what it has read, and the session's source manager, which keeps the files it loaded. In the session
that loaded the module, every dependency therefore hashes as it did when the module was loaded:
after the source is rewritten, isBinaryModuleUpToDate still returns true, and it returns true
for a module path where no file exists. A new session with the same search paths reads the files
and returns false for both. Its declaration in slang.h documents neither behaviour.

This reads the dependencies through a new CacheFileSystem over the same underlying file system and
a source manager of its own, as a new session reads them. A file system the caller supplied as a
CacheFileSystem is the caller's cache and is read as before. A regression test is added as
tools/slang-unit-test/unit-test-binary-module-up-to-date.cpp.

Measured on v2026.21: before, true for the rewritten source and for an absent path in the loading
session, false in a new one (win-x64, linux-x64, linux-arm64, osx-arm64); after, false in both.
